    "help.btn_forget": "Forget",
    "help.rules.body": "Needs drop slowly, and only while CodePet is on and you are at the computer. Breaks, nights away and Off never count. Feed, Water, Play and Bath fill a need back up. A need at 30% or less shows on Home, and an empty one on the bar too. Food and water are the needs that matter for safety: if one stays empty for 2 hours of active time, {name} needs you right away. Gentle (the default): after 6 more hours of active time {name} faints, and food or water wakes them up. Hardcore: {name} passes away instead and is remembered in Memories. Coding never costs care, and care never blocks coding. Finished coding work drops treats into a jar (up to 10, at most 12 an hour and 48 a day); each treat you give is 5 growth points, so {name} grows up after 16 treats and is fully grown after 80.",
    "help.pace.body": "Pace is steady. A playtest pace about 20 times faster exists for trying the whole cycle; it can be chosen with the codepet command line only.",
    "help.hardcore.body": "Hardcore: if food or water stays empty too long, {name} passes away instead of fainting and is remembered in Memories. Turning Hardcore on needs {name}'s name typed and a happy pet. Turning it off is one tap.",
    "help.helpers.body": "Count background helpers: when on, coding work that runs without a person at the keyboard (scripts, headless runs) shows a small robot in {name}'s thought bubble. It never earns treats or changes {name}'s face. It is off by default.",
    "help.forget.body": "Forget coding history deletes CodePet's record of coding sessions and events and clears the times of earned treats. Treats from the last day keep a time rounded to the hour, so the treat limits still count; CodePet clears it the first time it runs after that day. Treats still in the jar and growth already earned stay.",
    "help.apps.body": "Connect changes nothing until you tap it, and only for the apps you tick. Claude Code: CodePet installs a local plugin from its codepet-local marketplace with Claude Code's own command, which records it in ~/.claude/settings.json (enabledPlugins and extraKnownMarketplaces). The codepet-local marketplace also shows in Grok's marketplace tab; do not install it there. Codex: CodePet adds its hook lines to ~/.codex/hooks.json, and you trust them once in Codex with /hooks. While CodePet is Off, Codex still starts one login shell per event; Stop watching removes that cost. Grok: one file, ~/.grok/hooks/codepet.json. OpenCode: one file, ~/.config/opencode/plugins/codepet.js. Hermes: a plugin folder, ~/.hermes/plugins/codepet/, enabled with Hermes's own command, which rewrites ~/.hermes/config.yaml. Windows that were already open need a new window (Claude Code: or /reload-plugins). To disconnect by hand, remove those entries. Escape hatches: opencode --pure and hermes --safe-mode. Upgrades change hook behaviour inside the codepet program without another review in your apps; codepet doctor prints the bridge script's sha256.",
    "help.privacy.body": "CodePet only learns that a coding app started, worked, asked for you, finished or stopped. It keeps the app's name, the kind of event, counts, a few on/off flags, hashed ids (keyed with a secret stored on this computer) and times: events for 7 days, sessions for 30 days after they end, and treat times rounded to the hour after a day. It never stores or sends prompts, answers, tool names or contents, file paths, folder or project names, error text or model names, and it makes no network calls. Forget coding history deletes it, except the times of treats from the last day, rounded to the hour, which the treat limits need until that day is over.",
